# <PROJECT> code and test practices

`<project>/.retainer/practices.md`. Read by `code-reviewer` on the diff: a violation of a recorded practice
is a finding. Where the plan role is wired to read practices, they apply to the PLAN too: a divergence of the
plan from a recorded practice is a remark, a project prohibition is a contradiction.

Every entry is backed by a file of existing code, not by taste. A practice without a sample address
cannot be checked: the reader can neither confirm that this is really how the project writes, nor see
the whole form. A practice derived from taste lives until the first dispute; a practice with an address lives until
the file moves, and then it is updated.

What goes into the document is the RULE and the ADDRESS, not a code quotation. A quotation goes stale silently and starts teaching
a form that is no longer in the tree; an address goes stale visibly and gets updated.

## Entry form

    **<The rule in one phrase.>** <Why — what breaks without it.>
    Sample: <file>:<line>   ·   Distilled: <date>

- Distilled (the date the rule was taken from the code) is needed so staleness is visible to the
  reader: an entry three years old with an address that moved is more honest to delete than to patch
  at random.
- An address that no longer exists invalidates the entry. If you find one — update the address or delete
  the entry entirely; leaving a "rule without a sample" is not allowed, or the document becomes taste again.
- A rule from review is written here ONLY when it is about a class, not a one-off mistake. A one-off
  mistake goes to the project's error journal, not here.

